# Commands and results

- `pnpm build` and `pnpm check:generated`: passed.
- `pnpm lint` and `pnpm exec tsc --noEmit`: passed.
- Focused cross-layer regressions passed: Engine race safety, Classic root move, OpenSpec integration, Doctor, runtime assets (179 tests); app (425); Engine/workflow contract/Factory/integrations/Dashboard (279); Entry/Bundle/platform (478); Skill (347); repository (43); scripts (9); relevant Native root/config/workspace coverage (73).
- `git diff --check`: passed.

# Skipped checks

- The built-in `comet native check` produced receipt `runtime/evidence/check-receipts/0f5c37d7a22913fdd061305fe972a278f2c232abc21d4d1f287c8a768fadcbac.json` with `scan-limit` before scanning the 128-file partial implementation scope. It is not used as a passing receipt; the required repository checks above provide the concrete acceptance evidence.
- A single all-files Vitest invocation exceeds the host command window because `comet-scripts.test.ts` and the full Native suite are large. The affected runtime paths and all modified-module groups were run in bounded groups; CI remains responsible for its unrestricted complete invocation.

# Spec consistency

The implementation follows the three proposed specifications: shared workflow-contract configuration and path validation; Classic `legacy | docs` resolver plus safe explicit migration; independent Native `docs/comet` default. Classic and Native states, guards and runtimes remain separate.

# Known limitations and risks

- The implementation scope uses an explicit partial allowance because Native detail pagination summarized 40 unattributed changes; the allowance is bound to the current scope and cited in the change state.
- The built-in Native text checker has a fixed scan budget, so its scan-limit receipt is recorded rather than treated as proof of success.

# Conclusion

Pass. All 10 acceptance examples have concrete test or evaluation references, generated runtime assets are fresh, and no P0/P1 or uncovered mandatory behavior remains in the reviewed implementation.
